# xargs — real-world examples

Build and run commands from stdin — the glue between `find`/`grep` and
whatever needs to run on the results.

## Basics

```sh
# Find all .pdf files and remove them
find . -name '*.pdf' | xargs rm -f

# If filenames contain spaces, substitute with -I{} instead
find . -name '*.pdf' | xargs -I{} rm -f '{}'

# Safest with GNU find: null-delimited, handles any filename
find . -name '*.pdf' -print0 | xargs -0 rm -f
```

## Placement and batching

```sh
# -I{} places each item exactly where you want it
find . -name '*.pdf' | xargs -I{} -n1 echo '&{}='

# -n1 = one item per command invocation (-n2 = two at a time, etc.)
echo a b c d | xargs -n2 echo pair:
```

## Homelab uses

```sh
# Grep every cheatsheet for 'zypper' with filenames, safely
find ~/Public/ireaneus-cheatsheets -type f -print0 | xargs -0 grep -l zypper

# Restart several services in one line
echo plexmediaserver sshd | xargs -n1 sudo systemctl restart

# Parallel: checksum media files 4 at a time (-P = parallel jobs)
find /srv/media -name '*.mkv' -print0 | xargs -0 -P4 -n1 md5sum >> /tmp/media.md5

# Prompt before each deletion
find /tmp -name '*.old' | xargs -p rm

# Do nothing gracefully when input is empty (GNU)
grep -l 'TODO' *.md | xargs -r wc -l
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-I{}` | replace `{}` with each input item |
| `-n N` | at most N arguments per command |
| `-0` | null-delimited input (pair with `find -print0`) |
| `-P N` | run up to N commands in parallel |
| `-p` | prompt before each command |
| `-r` | don't run at all if input is empty (GNU) |
| `-t` | echo each command before running it |
